content:
Why it fits: an idle game needs ups and downs even while the player isn't looking, or they don't
reopen it. The director shapes a tension curve to pace "events worth coming back for."

Mapping the parts

Tempo	In an idle RPG
TensionModel	Session engagement / threat curve (flat = churn, ups and downs = return)
DirectorBrain phases	Calm (BuildUp) → boss appears / ambush rush (Peak) → reward tally & rest (Relax)
SeedAction	SpawnRaidBoss · TriggerGoldRush · AmbushEvent
WorldReaction	Resource/ecology chains (vein depletes → price shifts; monsters breed → field crowds) as a rule table
Telegraph	A "boss incoming" notice = push-notification re-engagement

Offline progress, settled in one pass

Idle games often don't tick every frame; they settle the "time away" in one pass on return. Run the three
Ticks over game time (accumulated elapsed) in big steps (e.g. 1 minute = 1 tick) to replay the phase
shifts and events that happened while away, compressed. The director only shapes the ups and downs (when a
boss showed up); the actual combat math is handled by your idle simulation.

// On return: replay the time away, compressed, in big steps
void CatchUp(float secondsAway)
{
    const float step = 60f; // 1 minute = 1 tick
    for (float t = 0; t < secondsAway; t += step)
    {
        _tension.Tick(playerPos, _world, step);
        _director.Tick(_world, _tension, playerPos, step); // paces boss/ambush events
        _world.Tick(step);
    }
    // Show accumulated ActiveEvents as a "here's what happened while you were gone" report.
}

Mobile checkpoints

Ups and downs = retention. Flat auto-progress drives churn; the director creates a reason to return with bosses/rushes.

Telegraph = push. Wire a "raid boss in 30 minutes" notice to a push notification to pull players back.

Battery-safe. Replay-on-return instead of per-frame simulation → zero background load.

Requirements

Unity 2022.3 or newer

No external dependencies (dependencies: {})

Install

Unity Package Manager → Add package from git URL:

https://github.com/achieveonepark/ach-tempo.git

Or directly in Packages/manifest.json:

{
  "dependencies": {
    "com.achieve.tempo": "https://github.com/achieveonepark/ach-tempo.git"
  }
}

Your first loop

Attach this component to an empty GameObject and a grass fire starts spreading.

using Achieve.Tempo.Director;
using Achieve.Tempo.Tension;
using Achieve.Tempo.World;
using UnityEngine;

public class TempoExample : MonoBehaviour
{
    WorldReaction _world;
    DirectorBrain _director;
    TensionModel  _tension;

    void Start()
    {
        _world = new WorldReaction(width: 64, height: 64, cellSize: 1f, origin: transform.position);
        _world.Fill("Grass");                       // lay down a grass field
        _world.SetWind(new Vector2(1f, 0f), 1.2f);  // wind blowing east

        _director = new DefaultDirectorBrain();
        _tension  = new TensionModel();
    }

    void Update()
    {
        float dt = Time.deltaTime;
        Vector3 player = transform.position;

        // Always the same order: tension → director → world
        _tension.Tick(player, _world, dt);
        _director.Tick(_world, _tension, player, dt);
        _world.Tick(dt);
    }
}

The order of the three Tick calls is always tension → director → world. The director reads this
frame's tension and seeds conditions; the world picks them up and spreads.

Feeding damage into tension

When the player is hit, spike the tension immediately.

void OnDamaged(float dmg, float maxHp) => _tension.OnPlayerDamaged(dmg, maxHp);

Swapping data

Rule tables, actions and thresholds all live in data centers with built-in defaults, so it runs with no
setup. To use your own values, just register them at startup.

using Achieve.Tempo.Config;

var rules = new RuleData();           // fill it yourself, or convert an SO loaded via Addressables
DataService<RuleData>.Register(rules);

Import the sample

Package Manager → Tempo → Samples → Basic Grass Fire. Import it and the
BasicGrassFireDirector component lets you watch tension, phase and spread on screen.

Next

World reaction for the spread rules in depth.

Director to customize phases and actions.

The director never spawns enemies. It reads a single tension number, picks a phase
(build-up → peak → relax), and then, within its budget, picks actions that seed only conditions into the world.

One seed action — SeedAction

public abstract class SeedAction
{
    public abstract float      Cost   { get; }  // how much budget it consumes
    public abstract SeedIntent Intent { get; }  // Prepare / Trigger / Calm
    public virtual  bool CanSeed(WorldReaction world, Vector3 playerPos) => true;
    public abstract void Seed   (WorldReaction world, Vector3 playerPos);
}

Intent ties to a phase — Prepare (build-up), Trigger (peak), Calm (relax). Each action
first checks CanSeed ("is it OK to seed now?") and only calls the world's entry points
(ApplyElement / SetWind).

The four built-ins:

Action	Intent	Role
DryUpwind	Prepare	Raises and dries the wind to set up good spread (no fire yet)
IgniteUpwind	Trigger	Throws an ember into dry grass upwind → it rides the wind toward the player
ChargeStorm	Trigger	Floats a storm cloud over metal/puddles to shock
SummonRain	Calm	Rains to quench and wet, giving a breather

The director's brain — DirectorBrain

public abstract class DirectorBrain
{
    public Phase CurrentPhase { get; }

    // swap the action list per game (virtual, not an interface)
    protected virtual IReadOnlyList<SeedAction> ActionPool
        => DataService<ActionData>.GetDataCenter().Actions;

    public void Tick(WorldReaction world, TensionModel tension, Vector3 playerPos, float dt)
    {
        Phase phase  = _fsm.Evaluate(tension.Current, dt);
        float budget = DataService<PhaseData>.GetDataCenter().BudgetFor(phase);

        foreach (SeedAction action in PickActions(phase, budget, world, playerPos))
            action.Seed(world, playerPos);   // ★ seeds conditions, never spawns enemies
    }

    protected virtual IEnumerable<SeedAction> PickActions(
        Phase phase, float budget, WorldReaction world, Vector3 playerPos) { /* default policy */ }
}

Use DefaultDirectorBrain to run with no overrides.

Phases and budget

Each phase gives the director a different budget, declared in PhaseData.

BuildUp — stack only good-spread conditions (dry grass, charge, fuel). Small budget, nothing dangerous.

Peak — on top of what's stacked, throw an ember so it all goes up. Spend a lot.

Relax — rain to quench and catch a breath while budget recovers. Calming actions only.

The default PickActions picks actions whose Intent matches the phase, within budget, that pass
CanSeed. Override it when you need finer policy.

public sealed class MyDirector : DirectorBrain
{
    protected override IReadOnlyList<SeedAction> ActionPool => _myActions;

    protected override IEnumerable<SeedAction> PickActions(
        Phase phase, float budget, WorldReaction world, Vector3 playerPos)
    {
        // game-specific priorities / cooldowns here
        return base.PickActions(phase, budget, world, playerPos);
    }
}

No flicker — PhaseFsm

With a single threshold the phase flickers at the boundary. So the enter and exit thresholds differ
(hysteresis), and a minimum relax time is guaranteed.

case Phase.BuildUp:
    if (tension >= Cfg.PeakEnter) _phase = Phase.Peak;              // e.g. 0.80
    break;
case Phase.Peak:
    if (tension <= Cfg.RelaxEnter) { _phase = Phase.Relax; _relaxTimer = 0; } // e.g. 0.45
    break;
case Phase.Relax:
    if (tension <= Cfg.BuildUpEnter && _relaxTimer >= Cfg.MinRelax)
        _phase = Phase.BuildUp;                                     // minimum rest guaranteed
    break;

All the director watches is one number: tension (0–1). TensionModel builds it in three steps:
normalize the two signals to 0–1 → combine → smooth.

The three steps

Normalize — bring player threat and nearby activity each into the 0–1 range.

Combine — if either is high the whole thing rises; if both are high it's near the max (never over 1).
The formula is 1-(1-a)(1-b).

Smooth — rise fast, fall slow. Danger should register immediately; relief should arrive gradually.

// 4) combine: either signal raises it; both → near max (never over 1)
float a   = Mathf.Clamp01(_playerStress * Cfg.StressWeight);
float b   = Mathf.Clamp01(around        * Cfg.AroundWeight);
float raw = 1f - (1f - a) * (1f - b);

// 5) smooth: rise fast, fall slow
float speed = raw > _smoothed ? Cfg.RiseSpeed : Cfg.FallSpeed;
float k = 1f - Mathf.Exp(-dt / Mathf.Max(1e-4f, speed));
_smoothed = Mathf.Lerp(_smoothed, raw, k);

Two ways to combine
Default: 1-(1-a)(1-b).

Sharper: just the larger of the two, Mathf.Max(a, b).

The key is "rise fast, fall slow." That's what makes the build-up → peak → relax flow feel natural.

Public surface

public class TensionModel
{
    public float Current { get; }          // what the director reads
    public void OnPlayerDamaged(float dmg, float maxHp); // spike on hit
    public void Tick(Vector3 playerPos, WorldReaction world, float dt);
}

Damage is immediate, threat is gradual

public void OnPlayerDamaged(float dmg, float maxHp)
    => _playerStress = Mathf.Clamp01(_playerStress + dmg / maxHp * Cfg.DamageGain);

Inside Tick, closer threats accumulate more, and stress decays gradually over time.

Injecting signals for tests

The two sample methods are virtual, so a test can inject signals directly without a physics scene
(swapped in place of an interface).

sealed class StubTensionModel : TensionModel
{
    public float ThreatSignal, AroundSignal;
    protected override float SampleThreat(Vector3 p) => ThreatSignal;
    protected override float SampleAround(Vector3 p, WorldReaction world) => AroundSignal;
}

The default SampleThreat counts nearby enemies via Physics.OverlapSphere; SampleAround weights
world.ActiveEvents so that closer, bigger events count more.

Tuning — TensionConfig

Strengths, radii, weights, speeds and thresholds are all data. Tune the ranges in your game.

Value	Meaning
DamageGain	Immediate spike on hit
ThreatGain / StressDecay	Threat accumulation / stress decay
StressWeight / AroundWeight	Weights when combining the two signals
RiseSpeed / FallSpeed	Time constants for rising / falling
ThreatRadius / AroundRadius	Sampling radii

The world reaction has no idea the director exists. Conditions come in, it spreads by rules, and it
keeps the list of live events up to date.

One cell's state — ElementalState

The current state of a cell (or object). It's a lightweight struct because the grid processes many of them.

public struct ElementalState
{
    public int   MaterialId;  // index into the material registry (-1 = empty)
    public float Temperature;
    public float Wetness;     // 0–1
    public float Charge;      // electric charge 0–1
    public bool  OnFire;
    public bool  Frozen;
}

Fixed properties — flammability, conductivity, melting point — aren't stored in the state; they're
looked up from MaterialDef.

MaterialDef

new MaterialDef("Grass", flammability: 0.9f, conductivity: 0.1f,
             ignitionPoint: 220f, freezePoint: 0f, waterAbsorption: 0.7f);

Materials are registered once and shared by index. RuleData.ResolveMaterialId(name) finds one by name,
creating and registering it if missing.

Reaction rule — ReactionRule

Reactions are data, not code: "this element / touching this material / over this threshold → changes like this".

[Serializable]
public sealed class ReactionRule
{
    public ElementTag Trigger;         // Fire/Water/Ice/Electric/Wind
    public string     TargetMaterials; // separated in one field: "Wood;Grass;Cloth"
    public StateEffect Effect;         // Ignite / Extinguish / Wet / Freeze ...
    public float      Probability;     // 0–1
    public float      Threshold;
}

Target materials go in one field separated by ;, not an array UI (a project convention). They're
split once and cached. Leave it empty to match every material.

The three BotW rules are the starting point:
an element changes a material — fire burns dry grass;

an element changes another element — water quenches fire;

depending on the situation an element creates a force — electricity shocks through metal.

The rule table comes from DataService<RuleData>.GetDataCenter().

Spread and results — WorldReaction

public class WorldReaction
{
    // what the director reads
    public IReadOnlyList<ReactionEvent> ActiveEvents { get; }

    // entry points where the director seeds conditions
    public void ApplyElement(Vector3 pos, float radius, ElementTag element, float power);
    public void SetWind(Vector2 dir, float strength);

    public void Tick(float dt); // spread by rules + refresh the list
}

How it spreads: rules are applied on a flat grid (a plain array / Dictionary, never a SortedSet)
so fire jumps to neighbor cells. It spreads each frame based on wind direction and how flammable the
neighbor is.

ActiveEvents: each entry is a ReactionEvent (position, magnitude, time-to-live, kind). The
director and tension model watch this list. Finished entries are cleared each frame, and nearby events
of the same kind merge into one.

public struct ReactionEvent
{
    public Vector3    Position;
    public float      Magnitude; // size (used when converting to tension)
    public float      Ttl;       // time left
    public ElementTag Kind;
}

Laying down a grid

var world = new WorldReaction(64, 64, cellSize: 1f, origin: transform.position);
world.Fill("Grass");            // all grass
world.SetCell(10, 12, "Water"); // one puddle cell
world.SetWind(new Vector2(1, 0), 1.2f);

Seeding conditions directly

world.ApplyElement(spot, radius: 2f, ElementTag.Fire, power: 1f);   // an ember
world.ApplyElement(playerPos, radius: 14f, ElementTag.Water, 1f);   // rain

ApplyElement applies the rule table to the touched cells immediately (water touching fire quenches it
right there); Tick then carries the spread forward.

Performance: grid math also fits Jobs/Burst + NativeArray. The default implementation uses a managed
flat array so it runs with no dependencies.
